EKA STANDARD
Version 1.0

The Engineering Knowledge Architecture (EKA) Standard. The normative source of
truth is the specification/ directory (the versioned snapshot of the standard
corpus). This file is a compact summary and index, not a definition: on any
conflict, specification/ wins over this file, README.md, the changelog, and the
contribution guide. "must" = binding requirement, "should" = recommendation,
"may" = option.

DESCRIPTION

EKA is the canonical conceptual model for engineering knowledge: the definition
of Artifact, Identity, State, knowledge taxonomies, the layer architecture, and
exchange contracts between systems. It represents engineering knowledge as
first-class entities (Artifacts) with a stable Identity, an explicit owned
State Vector, structured Content, and Relationships expressed by Identity. It
is a conceptual model, not a product or tool. Scope: concepts, invariants, and
contracts — not serialization formats, storage layout, or enforcement
mechanisms.

PRINCIPLES

P1  Separation of Concerns — knowledge and execution are separated into layers
    with explicit contracts.
P2  Explicit State — State is always explicit owned metadata, never implicit in
    structure.
P3  Stable Identity — Identity is immutable, independent of location, storage,
    State, and classification.
P4  Protocol vs Content Distinction — protocol belongs to the Operating Layer;
    Content to the Knowledge Layer.
P5  Layer Independence — each layer evolves without overhauling the others.
P6  Single Writer — every State field has exactly one owner; other views are
    projections.
P7  Forward-Only Transitions — State Domains move forward; corrections use a new
    instance + Relationship.
P8  Approved-Content Immutability — approved Content is not silently mutated.
P9  Structure as Projection of State — structure is derived from State, never a
    second fact.
P10 Two Change Channels — the Content (governance) and State (protocol)
    channels are separate.
P11 Determinism by Protocol — execution order is defined by protocol.
P12 Preservation Over Deletion — history is knowledge; superseded/archived are
    Records.
P13 Lossless Exchange — exchange loses and duplicates nothing.
P14 Minimum Canonical Core — the standard sets concepts and contracts;
    implementations choose mechanisms.
P15 Classification is Property, Not Identity — a classification change never
    breaks references.
P16 Enforcement Capability Varies, Invariants Don't — mechanisms differ; the
    invariants are identical.

RULES

 1. R0  Structural well-formedness — artifacts must be structurally parseable with valid identity fields. (See: specification/rules/rule-001.md)
 2. R1  Identity uniqueness — no duplicate (Namespace, Type, ID, InstanceVersion). (See: specification/rules/rule-002.md)
 3. R2  Identity canonical — Identity serialized losslessly/unambiguously/machine-parseably, never encoded in location or filename. (See: specification/rules/rule-003.md)
 4. R3  State value validity — every owned State Domain value is a member of its value set. (See: specification/rules/rule-004.md)
 5. R4  Owned-set compliance — present State fields equal exactly the type's owned State Domains. (See: specification/rules/rule-005.md)
 6. R5  Referential integrity — every Relationship reference resolves; draft tolerance for Draft. (See: specification/rules/rule-006.md)
 7. R6  Classification — primary Knowledge Dimension declared and valid; Domain derived from Type. (See: specification/rules/rule-007.md)
 8. R7  Change Log consistency — every transition has an entry; last entry equals current value. (See: specification/rules/rule-008.md)
 9. R8  Single-writer & projection non-writer — one writer per State field; projections never write. (See: specification/rules/rule-009.md)
10. R9  Well-formedness — Content structure matches the type family; superseded ADR declares successor. (See: specification/rules/rule-010.md)
11. R10 Stratification traceability — non-stratum-1 artifacts trace to a higher stratum (warning). (See: specification/rules/rule-011.md)
12. R11 Domain coherence — declared Domain is canonical and equals the Type's home domain. (See: specification/rules/rule-012.md)
13. R12 Cross-stratum supersession prohibition — supersedes/amends never target a higher stratum. (See: specification/rules/rule-013.md)
14. R13 Graph pass / note resolution gates — work-item note-resolution gates at in-review/done. (See: specification/rules/rule-014.md)

Verdicts: conformant | conformant with warnings | non-conformant. Validators are
mechanical, deterministic, read-only, and derive verdicts only from this
standard (P16).

DOMAINS

Discovery (stratum 1) — intent, requirements, research. Tokens: vis-, str-, req-, fnd-.
Architecture (stratum 2) — decisions, specifications, standards, vocabulary. Tokens: arc-, adr-, dec-, spec-, std-, gls-.
Planning (stratum 3) — commitment: scope, epics, plans, traceability. Tokens: scp-, epc-, plan-, trc-.
Execution (stratum 4) — work items, containers, tickets, reviews, sessions, notes. Tokens: rvw-, ctr-, tkt-, sto-, ts-, bug-, td-, ch-, spk-, ses-, cmt-.
Operations (stratum 5) — runbooks, release records. Tokens: run-, rel-.

Stratum Authority Invariant: lower-stratum knowledge must not contradict
higher-stratum knowledge in force; lower-stratum artifacts never supersede or
amend higher-stratum artifacts.

CANONICAL TOKENS (27)

Discovery:      vis- (Vision/Manifesto), str- (Strategy), req- (Requirement/PRD), fnd- (Research Finding)
Architecture:   arc- (Architecture Description), adr- (ADR), dec- (Decision Record), spec- (Specification), std- (Standard/Guideline), gls- (Glossary/Term)
Planning:       scp- (Scope Definition), epc- (Epic), plan- (Plan), trc- (Traceability Artifact)
Execution:      rvw- (Review), ctr- (Execution Container), tkt- (Ticket), sto- (Story), ts- (Task), bug- (Bug), td- (Technical Debt), ch- (Chore), spk- (Spike), ses- (Session), cmt- (Note)
Operations:     run- (Runbook/Operational Guide), rel- (Release Record)

The token is part of Identity (the Type qualifier), never
methodology-dependent. Methodology terms (PRD, ADR, RFC, Epic, Sprint, Ticket,
Release, Incident, Runbook, etc.) are Representation Aliases, never Artifact
types. Operating tokens (ctr-, tkt-, sto-, ts-, bug-, td-, ch-, spk-, ses-,
cmt-) share "operating token (informational dimension)" semantics.

